# Trivy findings accepted as design decisions or upstream constraints.
# Format: one rule ID per line. Add a comment above each entry explaining
# why the finding is accepted and what would need to change to fix it.
#
# Re-evaluate this file when:
#   • the upstream postgres image changes its built-in UID/GID
#   • the kube-phoenix RBAC model is split into read-only and mutating roles
#   • Trivy's trusted-registry policy gains support for ghcr.io

# postgres:alpine ships with a built-in 'postgres' user at UID/GID 70. The
# container cannot start with runAsUser/runAsGroup > 10000 without rebuilding
# the image. The container still runs non-root with all capabilities dropped,
# read-only root filesystem and seccomp=RuntimeDefault.
AVD-KSV-0020
AVD-KSV-0021

# init-permissions runs as UID 0 to chown a freshly provisioned PV. fsGroup
# alone is not honoured by every CSI driver, so root is required for the
# one-shot mkdir/chown/chmod. The container drops all capabilities, mounts
# only the data volume, and has allowPrivilegeEscalation=false.
AVD-KSV-0012

# The kube-phoenix ClusterRole intentionally grants write verbs on workload
# resources (deployments, statefulsets, deployments/scale, statefulsets/scale,
# pods/eviction) — scaling and pod eviction are core product features. Node
# delete is required for cluster downscaling. See helm/kube-phoenix/templates/
# clusterrole.yaml for the inline rationale.
AVD-KSV-0048

# The chart's default image is ghcr.io/macxsimilian/kube-phoenix — our own
# registry. Trivy's KSV-0125 trusted-registry list is configurable and does
# not include ghcr.io by default.
AVD-KSV-0125
